---
import MainLayout from '../layouts/MainLayout.astro';
import { productionUrl, resourceLinks, sharingTargets } from '../data/site';

export const formatCards = [
  {
    title: 'RSS/Atom Feeds',
    icon: 'https://www.rss.style/favicon.svg',
    textHtml:
      'RSS and Atom <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Web_feed">news feeds</a> are a great way to get updates from your favorite sites.',
    url: 'https://www.rss.style/',
    demo: 'https://www.fileformat.info/news/rss.xml'
  },
  {
    title: 'Sitemaps',
    icon: '/sitemap/favicon.svg',
    textHtml:
      '<a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sitemaps">Sitemaps</a> (<code>sitemap.xml</code> files) help search engines index your site.',
    url: '/sitemap/',
    demo: '/sitemap/demo.xml'
  },
  {
    title: 'Outlines',
    icon: '/opml/favicon.svg',
    textHtml:
      '<a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/OPML">OPML Outlines</a> are a great way to share the sites in your news feed.',
    url: '/opml/',
    demo: '/opml/#demos',
    demoText: 'Demos'
  }
];

---

<MainLayout
  title="XML.Style: Make your XML files look better with Javascript"
  h1="XML Styling with Javascript"
  path="/"
  shareTargets={sharingTargets}
  productionUrl={productionUrl}
>
  <h2>What is this?</h2>

  <p>
    Most websites have several <code>.xml</code> files that are used by search engines and other
    services to index and follow the site.
  </p>

  <p>
    By default, these files look horrible when a human looks at them in a browser. But there is a
    simple way to make them look better: includes a single Javascript in your XML file!
  </p>

  <div class="row row-cols-1 row-cols-md-3">
    {
      formatCards.map((format) => (
        <div class="col">
          <div class="card m-3" style="width: 20rem;">
            <div class="card-body p-0">
              <h3 class="card-title p-3 border-bottom my-0">
                <img src={format.icon} alt={format.title} class="pe-2" style="height: 1.5em;" />
                {format.title}
              </h3>
              <div class="card-text px-3 py-2" set:html={format.textHtml} />
              <div class="card-footer d-flex justify-content-between">
                <a class="btn btn-outline-primary btn-sm" href={format.url}>
                  Instructions
                </a>
                <a class="btn btn-outline-primary btn-sm" href={format.demo}>
                  {format.demoText ?? 'Demo'}
                </a>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      ))
    }
  </div>

  <h2 id="how">How do I use it?</h2>

  <p>
    You need to include a &lt;script&gt; tag in your XML file.
  </p>

  <p>
    At the top of your XML file, it will look like:
  </p>
  <pre class="m-4 p-3 bg-light border rounded">
&lt;?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?&gt;
&lt;data-depends-on-type&gt;
...
  </pre>

  <p>
    Add a new third line so it looks like:
  </p>
  <pre class="m-4 p-3 bg-light border rounded">
&lt;?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?&gt;
&lt;data-depends-on-type&gt;
&lt;script src="https://www.xml.style/<i>xml-file-type</i>/basic.js" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml"&gt;&lt;/script&gt;
...
  </pre>

<p>
  Each type of XML file will have a different script, so replace <code><i>xml-file-type</i></code> with either <code>sitemap</code> or <code>opml</code>.
</p>

  <h2 id="warn">Gotchas</h2>

  <p>A couple key points that I learned the hard way:</p>

  <ul>
    <li class="py-1">You must serve the page with the content type <code>text/xml</code>.</li>
    <li class="py-1">The namespace declaration (<code>xmlns=</code> above) must be <i>exactly</i> as above.</li>
    <li class="py-1">You must have the <code>&lt;/script&gt;</code>.  You cannot have just the opening tag even
        if it ends with <code>/&gt;</code>.
  </ul>

  <p>
    Otherwise various browsers will either fail to run the script or complain about invalid XML.
  </p>

</MainLayout>
